The Racket binding gives you a Goblins-shaped `session` surface with native
values
([`pancake.rkt`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/racket/pancake.rkt)).
The transport modules are `pancake-tcp.rkt`, `pancake-inproc.rkt`, and lazy
`pancake-iroh.rkt`. The [Bindings]($link.page('bindings')) page covers install,
and [modules]($link.page('modules')) has the actor vocabulary.

## The same round-trip

The [Start building]($link.page('start')) round-trip (connect, fetch, send,
handle the reply) reads like this in Racket. The local is `sess` so it does not
shadow `session-fetch` / `session-send`.

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt")
>
> ;; tcp: the peer is a place, a cleartext host and port.
> (define partner (tcp-locator "127.0.0.1" 9099))
>
> (define sess (connect partner))
> (define greeter (session-fetch sess "greeter")) ; a capability, by name
> (printf "~a\n" (session-send sess greeter 'greet "world"))
> (session-close! sess)
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt"
>          "pancake-iroh.rkt")
>
> ;; iroh reachability: the peer is a key, not a place. IROH-REACH-DISCOVERY
> ;; resolves the bare node id over the open internet; no address in hand.
> (define partner "ocapn://3b6a…da29.iroh")
>
> (define sess (connect partner IROH-REACH-DISCOVERY))
> (define greeter (session-fetch sess "greeter")) ; a capability, by name
> (printf "~a\n" (session-send sess greeter 'greet "world"))
> (session-close! sess)
> ```

The iroh snippet changes only the locator: a bare node id plus the reachability
posture. `session-fetch` and `session-send` are unchanged. For dial-by-id and
discovery, see [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')).

## A Goblins-faithful actor layer

Racket keeps the Goblins verbs intact. The verbs are Goblins's; the substrate
is pancake. This is not a Goblins compatibility layer, and an existing Goblins
program will not run on it unchanged. You define an actor constructor with
`(define (^name bcom arg ...) ...)`, return its `methods` behavior, and then
`spawn` it. You call it near with `$`, send it eventually with `<-` or `<-np`,
observe a promise with `on`, and become a new behavior with `bcom`, all inside
`with-vat`. On top of that, `connect`, `session-fetch`, and `session-send` reach
a partner, and `(accept (listen "127.0.0.1" 9099))` hands back the same
`session` that `connect` returns.

> **`$` and `<-`**, near-call and eventual-send, come straight from the
> [E language](http://erights.org/), and [The modules]($link.page('modules'))
> page defines both. Across a session only `<-` works, since a partner's object
> is never in your vat.

## Async

Concurrency is Racket's own green threads and ports. `(session-serve sess)`
pumps the inbound loop, so run it on its own green thread, and `(on vow ...)`
runs a callback when the vow (Goblins's word for a promise) settles.
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers the CapTP ideas.

## Binding check and example

```sh
zig build racket-dogfood    # the Racket consumer against the pancake archives
```

The full surface lives in the
[Racket binding README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/racket/README.md),
and of the four bindings this is the one that keeps Goblins's own verbs, so
the
[rooms chat client](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/apps/rooms/racket/room-client.rkt)
reads almost like Goblins code.
